// =====================================================================
// Solution C - date dimension, second role
//
// Lori's point on the call: the relevant date differs per dataset (the
// forecast uses close date, not created date). Holistics allows only ONE
// ACTIVE relationship between any given pair of models, so a second date
// field needs a second model to point at.
//
// This is that model. It is the whole thing — a label and an extend.
// Every fiscal column is inherited from `seed_c_dates` and re-binds
// automatically, because none of them names a model: they are @sql over
// `{{ #SOURCE.col }}` and bare `{{ fiscal_year }}`. Nothing to
// re-declare, nothing to keep in sync. Change the fiscal calendar in
// `seed_c_dates` and every role model follows.
//
// The alternative Holistics documents for this — an inactive
// relationship reached with `with_relationships()` inside each metric —
// costs one clause per metric per role. That scales with metrics x
// roles. This scales with roles alone, which is the right shape for a
// customer who wants fiscal year applied across a lot of metrics.
//
// Here the second role is demonstrated on `ecommerce_users.sign_up_date`
// because the demo orders table only carries one date. For Seed it is
// the same three lines pointed at a second date on the same fact — two
// active relationships from one model to two different date models is
// fine, the one-active rule is per model PAIR.
//
// Naming: give each role a label that says which date it describes, not
// which table it came from. Users see 'Signup Date' and 'Order Date',
// never two things both called 'Fiscal Year'.
// =====================================================================
Model seed_c_dates_signup = seed_c_dates.extend({
  label: 'Signup Date'
  description: 'Date dimension in its signup-date role. Same Oct-Sep fiscal calendar as Seed Dates (Solution C), joined on user signup instead of order date.'
})
